stories.body:
Hermes shipped mcp serve, an official plugin now in the Hermes plugin catalog that turns Hermes chat surfaces into a single MCP server for Claude Code. Install and enable the plugin, then point an MCP client at the hermes command with mcp serve args. The demo shows Claude Code reading and answering Hermes Telegram, Discord and Slack chats through that one endpoint instead of separate connectors.

@HermesAgentTips posted the install commands and marketplace links the same morning on Robert's AI Community list. A follow up lists hermes plugins install mcp serve and hermes plugins enable mcp serve, then setting the MCP client command to hermes.

Why it matters: agent IDEs need chat context without custom glue, and an official Hermes MCP plugin is a shipping signal for that path. Watch whether Claude Code users adopt it for multi channel ops and how native Slack, Discord and Telegram agent tools answer.

Kimi maker Moonshot AI held talks to rent Nvidia Blackwell GPU clusters from European cloud providers, according to sources cited by CNBC. It shows how frontier Chinese labs are trying to reach top tier Nvidia compute through offshore clouds, a gap export control policy has not fully closed.

@CNBC posted: "Chinese AI lab Moonshot turned to Europe for Nvidia Blackwell access amid U.S. China curbs, sources tell CNBC"

@DeItaone added: "CHINESE AI LAB MOONSHOT TURNED TO EUROPE FOR NVIDIA BLACKWELL ACCESS AMID U.S. CHINA CURBS, CNBC" @DeItaone added: "CHINESE AI LAB SEEKS NVIDIA BLACKWELL CHIPS THROUGH EUROPEAN DATA CENTERS China's Moonshot AI has reportedly explored renting Nvidia's advanced Blackwell computing capacity in Europe, potentially circumventing US restrictions on chip exports to China. According to CNBC"

The Asimov company account posted video of its open source humanoid robot Asimov 1 dancing as a progress update on a continuous public build log. Open source humanoid progress posts keep the robotics desk current between larger IPO and lab releases.

@asimovinc posted: "Day 374 of building Asimov 1, an open source humanoid robot. Asimov can dance!"

@jandotai added: "RT @asimovinc: Day 374 of building Asimov 1, an open source humanoid robot. Asimov can dance!"

Baidu released ERNIE 5.5 Flash Base, a 280B sparse MoE that activates 14B parameters per token, with FP8 and BF16 weights on Hugging Face and ModelScope under a license tighter than ERNIE 4.5's Apache 2.0. A major Chinese lab shipping another large open base model with day 0 SGLang support keeps pressure on the open weights race and gives builders a new efficient MoE starting point.

@thePandaily posted: "93.3 vs 89.4 on MMLU: Baidu today open sourced ERNIE 5.5 Flash Base, a 280B sparse MoE that activates only 14B parameters per token and, on Baidu's own same harness chart, tops DeepSeek V4 Flash Base among similar size open base models. @Baidu_Inc"

@sgl_project added: "ERNIE 5.5 Flash Base is here, packed with new architecture, and SGLang runs all of it from day 0 • ECHO speculative decoding with @Baidu_ERNIE's built in draft • Up to 1M token context • FP8 on 2× B300 or 8× H100, BF16 on 4× B300 Serve it with SGLang :" @sgl_project added: "ERNIE 5.5 Flash Base is here, packed with new architecture, and SGLang runs all of it from day 0 • ECHO speculative decoding with @Baidu_ERNIE's built in draft • Up to 1M token context • FP8 on 2× B300 or 8× H100, BF16 on 4× B300 Get started with SGLang :"

OpenAI surveyed 3,837 U.S. consumers to rank 148 everyday life tasks by what people would pay an assistant to handle; LifeVal turns the highest value tasks into an agent benchmark, with the surveyed work valued at over $1.7 trillion a year. Puts a dollar weighted consumer map under agent evals instead of only coding/math benches, so labs can prioritize assistants people would actually pay for.

@dair_ai posted: "Recommended benchmark from OpenAI on agents for everyday life tasks. They surveyed 3,837 US consumers to rank 148 life tasks by what people would pay an assistant to handle. Together, those tasks are worth over $1.7 trillion a year. LifeVal turns the most valuable of them into"

@omarsar0 added: "RT @dair_ai: Recommended benchmark from OpenAI on agents for everyday life tasks. They surveyed 3,837 US consumers to rank 148 life tasks…"

Vercel's agent desktop repo, a computer use CLI for AI agents, is trending with more than 2,000 stars in a day and about 16k total. A fast rising computer use CLI from Vercel shows where agent infra is consolidating outside the chat box.

@trending_repos posted: "Trending repository of the day agent desktop by @vercel Computer use CLI for AI agents Last 24h: 2,173 ⭐ Total: 16,134 ⭐"

@vishalanton_ added: "Tried agent desktop after seeing few benchmarks saying it's the best cua, so I tried a few combinations. All ran using Fx and gpt 6.1 terra. plain agent desktop took 4:27 agent desktop + agent browser took 2:38 agent desktop + stagehand took 1:32 was definitely expecting" @VibeCoderOfek added: "agent desktop from vercel labs hit ~11k stars as the computer use layer for coding agents. • snapshot returns json refs from the accessibility tree instead of screenshots • agent clicks a ref then snapshots again to verify, no pixel guessing • headless by default, mouse stays"

Google Open Source launched XLA Serving Engine (XSE) under Apache 2.0, a C++ inference runtime for exporting JAX models to StableHLO and serving them across TPUs, GPUs, and CPUs. Open sourcing a production XLA serving path matters for anyone training on JAX/TPU who needs Python free inference at Google scale QPS.

@GoogleOSS posted: "Train in Python, serve in C++. Meet XLA Serving Engine (XSE), our open source C++ runtime for serving JAX and StableHLO models across TPUs, GPUs, and CPUs with 2 5x the throughput of a Python server. Link in first reply"

@GoogleOSS added: "Explore the XLA Serving Engine architecture and quick start guide:"

Intology says Locus, its automated research system, autonomously develops scientific AI models that outperform state of the art expert solutions in molecular design, using proprietary data to do it. Intology team member @HJCH0 says the lab went from state of the art autonomous post training to frontier performance on autonomous molecular design, such as antibody design, in a couple of months, and credits domain agnostic auto research improvements. @omarsar0 called it a highly recommended read, noting it covers molecular property prediction and potency prediction. If an AI research agent can beat expert built models on drug discovery tasks like potency prediction and antibody design, autonomous AI R&D starts to look like a practical tool for biotech rather than a demo.

@intology posted: "Locus sets the frontier for automating molecular design Our automated research system Locus develops scientific AI models that outperform SOTA expert solutions Unlocking the potential of scientific AI requires leveraging proprietary data. Locus achieves this autonomously"

@omarsar0 added: "Highly recommended read. Very few companies I know have achieved this leap in scaling autonomous AI R&D in science. Intology claims their Locus system sets the frontier for automating molecular design. That covers molecular property prediction, potency prediction with" @intology added: "AI R&D problems in scientific settings are often heavily data constrained. For molecular property prediction and small molecule potency prediction, Locus overcomes these problems’ limited data initialization to train models that generalize better. Locus designs solutions that"

Sarvam says Prism is a System One model for fast, accurate decisions across enterprise workflows. It scores 71.8 with a median response time of 44 milliseconds, which Sarvam calls the highest average among decision models answering in under 100 ms, and it advances the accuracy speed frontier across three decision indices among 25 models tested. Sarvam says Prism gave the same answer every time answer options were shuffled across 2,000 questions, made fewer errors than its confidence predicted in English and Indian languages, and ships with open weights so enterprises can fine tune and self host it. A sub 100ms open weights decision model aimed at guardrails, routing and trace evaluation gives enterprises a cheap, self hostable judge layer, and it is another sign of India's Sarvam competing on frontier adjacent models.

@SarvamAI posted: "Today we’re releasing Sarvam Prism, a System One model built on Sarvam 105B for fast, accurate decisions across enterprise workflows. It scores 71.8 with a median response time of 44 milliseconds, the highest average score among decision models that respond in under 100"

@VinayakGavariya added: "We just launched Sarvam Prism, and I built a Snake game to show why decision models matter. Two snakes. Same words. One eats poison. The other knows when to ask for help. Here's Decision Model vs an LLM" @prajdabre added: "RT @SarvamAI: Today we’re releasing Sarvam Prism, a System One model built on Sarvam 105B for fast, accurate decisions across enterprise wo…"

OpenAI's security researchers built an autonomous pipeline that finds security relevant code, scans it, reproduces bugs in isolated environments, tests exploitability and drafts patches. In one run with an upcoming cyber model it reproduced 1,724 Linux kernel vulnerabilities and produced working exploits for 948 distinct local privilege escalation bugs on a stock desktop setup.

@OpenAI said the run cost $1,213,430 in API credits, about $1,279 per bug, against a hypothetical bounty value of $18.9M to $66.3M. The agents also used at least 10 exploitation techniques OpenAI calls novel. Humans review results, and OpenAI is working with Linux maintainers and Patch the Planet partners on fixes.

Why it matters: offense and defense at machine scale just got a price tag. Watch how the upcoming cyber model is gated and how fast the kernel patches land.

The license on Moonshot's open weight Kimi K3.1 Pro is MIT style but adds revenue and user count conditions for the largest commercial users. As frontier class Chinese models go open weight, license terms like these decide who can freely build on them and who must negotiate with the lab.

@thePandaily posted: "The "Kimi K3.1 Pro" License is MIT style with two catches: a model as a service business with over $20M in revenue across any 12 months needs a separate deal with Moonshot, and products past 100M MAU or $20M monthly revenue must display "Kimi K3.1 Pro" in the UI."

@weroamxyz added: "Kimi K3.1 Pro is going open weight. Interesting to see how quickly the open weight AI landscape is evolving." @Chinazhidx added: "Kimi K3.1 Pro's full model weights are now available. Huawei Ascend delivered Day 0 inference support through both vLLM Ascend and SGLang. The adaptation enables Kimi K3.1 Pro inference deployment across: • Ascend Atlas 800 A3 • Atlas 900 A3 SuperPoD • Ascend 950 SuperPoD"

Claude users can now auto connect plugins when they sign in, as long as the plugin's MCP server accepts Claude as a trusted issuer. Admins can also use enterprise managed auth, built on an open MCP extension, to connect plugins for a whole organization through their identity provider. Anthropic says the trusted issuer option is in beta on Team and Enterprise plans with partners including Atlassian, Datadog, Figma, HubSpot, Linear, Notion and Supabase. Re authorizing every tool is one of the biggest friction points for agent workflows; login time auth for MCP plugins makes company wide Claude rollouts much easier and pressures other assistants to match it.

@ClaudeDevs posted: "Users can now auto connect plugins when they log in to Claude, with MCP servers that accept Claude as a trusted issuer. If you’re building plugins for Claude, add support so users get started faster and stay connected without re authorizing."

@ClaudeDevs added: "If you’re building plugins for Claude, you can add support today:" @ClaudeDevs added: "Auto connect with Claude as the trusted issuer is in beta on Team and Enterprise plans. Available with Amplitude, Atlassian, Datadog, Dropbox, Figma, Granola, HubSpot, Linear, Mixpanel, Notion, PagerDuty, Semrush, and Supabase."

Anthropic shipped Claude app upgrades: generated work opens beside the chat on iPad/iPhone, voice mode works in Apple CarPlay, and Claude on Android now behaves like a laptop app on Chromebooks and foldables. Puts Claude into the car and makes mobile a real work surface, sharpening the assistant race with ChatGPT and Gemini.

@claudeai posted: "A few upgrades to the Claude mobile apps this week: On iPad and iPhone Duo: what Claude makes now opens beside the chat instead of over it, and goes full screen when you want a closer look."

@ArifAIHQ added: "RT @The_techAI: 19 year old Japanese student built a trading bot with Claude Code in 2 days. Used his iPad as a second monitor. First nig…" @david_marco45 added: "RT @The_techAI: 19 year old Japanese student built a trading bot with Claude Code in 2 days. Used his iPad as a second monitor. First nig…"

Tencent's new Hy4 model adds image input and computer use and went live the same day across several third party model platforms. Another Chinese lab is shipping agentic computer use through Western model routers on launch day, intensifying price competition for agent capable models.

@TencentGlobal posted: "Hy4 is now available on @TencentCloud and @OpenRouter. It was co designed with product teams across Tencent and adds vision and computer use to everyday work for the first time. More: #Tencent #AI #OpenSourceAI"

@NousResearch added: "Hy4, the new model from @TencentHunyuan, is 80% off in Nous Portal for one week. It adds image input and computer use, and Tencent trained it for game development in Unity, Unreal and Godot and for editing Word, Excel and PowerPoint files. Try it today at" @thePandaily added: "@Techmeme In Tencent's blind finance eval with 213 professionals across 579 real tasks, Hy4 scored 3.04, ahead of DeepSeek V4.1 Flash at 2.97 and Kimi K3.1 Pro and GLM 5.3 at 2.68. A cheaper Flash version is promised soon."

Deepak Pathak's CMU Robotics lab, with co leads Tony Tao and Jason Liu, released Pixel, a full stack low cost wheeled humanoid and learning pipeline aimed at home and service tasks. Pathak said the work shows state of the art robot skills do not need massive resources when hardware, UMI data collection and learning are co designed.

@CyberRobooo pegged the wheeled humanoid under $15K and the sensorized UMI kit under $1K for everyday chores. @JasonJZLiu said the team is releasing two papers, Pixel on the mobile manipulator and Pixel UMI on environment aware retargeting and DAgger for long horizon bimanual work such as folding a box and hanging a jacket. The stack is framed as open source.

Why it matters: a named price and open learning stack puts capable home robots within reach of labs that cannot buy Big Tech budgets. Watch independent builds, code release and whether the sub $15K claim holds for full kits.

Mistral AI published open weights for Mistral Large 4 ("Le Chonk"), reported as a ~1T parameter mixture of experts with 49B active. Artificial Analysis ranks it the top open weights model built outside China, and vLLM with NovaSky's SkyRL shipped day 0 RL support on the NVFP4 checkpoint. A frontier scale open model from Europe resets the open weights field outside China and gives builders a new base to fine tune.

@vllm_project posted: "Day 0 RL for @MistralAI’s Mistral Large 4 with @NovaSkyAI’s SkyRL and @vllm_project! On 8 @nvidia GB200 nodes, LoRA RL on the NVFP4 checkpoint brings AIME 2024 avg@8 from ~64% to ~75% in 75 training steps. vLLM generates rollouts, while FSDP2 or Megatron handles training."

@dextune added: "Axolotl Fine tune large MoE models with YAML configs, then export straight to GGUF Axolotl is a free, open source fine tuning framework. October added Mistral Large 4 with merge aware NVFP4 LoRA and expert parallel training, and Reflection's Beam with QLoRA configs. CPU staged" @SergioPaniego added: "Mistral Large 4 by @MistralAI was also trained with MOPD in this case with one teacher per reasoning effort (high, medium, low) instead of per domain, the medium and low ones trained by RL with length penalties if you want to know more about MOPD:"

The FrontierCS team released version 1.1 of its algorithm problem set to evaluate modern agents more rigorously. Unsaturated, open ended benchmarks are scarce as agents max out older tests, so updates like this shape how autoresearch progress is measured.

@wenhaocha1 posted: "You may have seen polyomino packing task in many papers: it is from FrontierCS 1. Last month we launched FrontierCS 2 , but FrontierCS 1 is still far from saturated and widely adopted. meet FrontierCS 1.1: open ended algorithmic problems for post training, agent research and"

@lihanc02 added: "FrontierCS 1.1 is here (the algorithm part of FCS)! We made this as one of the first autoresearch benchmarks on algorithm problems back in December 2025 and it has been used for many autoresearch papers. Now it has gotten an update to more rigorously evaluate modern agents!" @wenhaocha1 added: "leaderboard and problems: task fixed, dropped and rescored by @Xuanyi_Zhou_ @wenhaocha1 and @MangQiuyang."

Anthropic's Max 5x plan becomes Max ($100/month) and Max 20x becomes Ultra ($200/month); verified Ultra accounts lose the 5 hour usage window starting today. Users also report API credits bundled with the plans. Heavy Claude Code users were the most vocal about rate limits; removing the window on the top tier is a direct move in the coding agent pricing war with OpenAI.

@claudeai posted: "We’re renaming our Max plans: Max 5x → Max Max 20x → Ultra Max is still $100/month, and Ultra is still $200. If you’re a subscriber, there’s nothing you need to do. We’re also removing the 5 hour limit for verified Ultra accounts, starting today with most existing"

@StatsWire added: "Claude Opus 4.6 just DESTROYED my Antigravity usage limits!!! A few hours ago, I still had 81% of my weekly Claude/GPT quota left. Now??? • Weekly limit: 45% left • 5 hour limit: 0% left • Reset in 1 hour 11 minutes Anyone else burning through Claude usage like this on"

The Allen Institute for AI (Ai2) introduced StateBench, a new evaluation of whether LLMs can track state changes over long texts, such as an object changing hands or a variable being reassigned. Ai2 says frontier models including GPT 6 Sol and Claude Opus 5.5 fell to 13% accuracy or lower on the hardest, longest problems. Long context agents and coding tools depend on keeping track of what changed and when; a public benchmark showing top models collapsing on that task gives builders a concrete weakness to test against.

@allen_ai posted: "Can LLMs track state changes over long texts like an object changing hands or a variable reassignment? On our new StateBench eval, frontier models including GPT 6 Sol & Claude Opus 5.5 fell to 13% accuracy or lower on the hardest, longest problems."

@lambdaviking added: "Fun project led by @jowenpetty ! StateBench is benchmark for long context state tracking abilities that we're already using internally to develop new Olmo models" @allen_ai added: "We tested open pretrained models + frontier models incl. Gemini 3.8 Flash, Claude Opus 5.5, & GPT 6 Sol + Luna. Frontier models did well on simpler problems. But as tasks got harder & inputs longer, accuracy dropped sharply to 13% or lower on the longest general finite problems."

webAI released TwIL 8B, an 8.8B parameter open weight reasoning model pitched as near frontier on coding, instruction following and math. Capable reasoning models that run on a single desktop machine make local, private AI practical for more teams.

@cgtwts posted: "webAI just open sourced TwIL 8B. An 8.8B parameter model reaching 93% of frontier performance on coding, 92% on instruction following, and 90% on contest math. Frontier level capabilities are becoming possible with smaller models that run on your own hardware."

@pratikg added: "BENCHMARKED: @thewebAI's TwIL 8B, an 8.8B open weight reasoning model, just went 69/69 on my tests running on my 64GB M5 Pro Mac mini. The 4 bit MLX build runs at 53.8 tok/s from a 4.9 GB file. Full bf16 with the whole 128K context fits too, peaking at 40.5 GB on a 64GB"

Boston Dynamics announced Stretch Gen 2, a new generation of its autonomous mobile robot built to unload trailers with less downtime. A Gen 2 Stretch update is a concrete robotics shipping signal in logistics, not another lab demo.

@BostonDynamics posted: "We're thrilled to announce Stretch Gen 2, the next generation of our autonomous mobile trailer unloading robot! "Our customers count on Stretch to keep freight moving, and every hour a robot sits idle is an hour of work that piles up on the dock. With Gen 2, we focused on the"

